# What must never reach a container image.
#
# There was no .dockerignore, the builder stage is `COPY . .`, and Next's
# standalone output copies `.env` files on purpose — it loads them itself at
# runtime. So the shipped image carried /app/.env and /app/.env.local, holding
# SESSION_SECRET, ENCRYPTION_KEY, DATABASE_URL, POSTGRES_PASSWORD and the market
# data key. That is every session forgeable and every MT5 investor password
# decryptable, to anyone who can read the image.
#
# It never needed to be there. docker-compose passes each of those to the
# container through `environment:`, which is where the running app reads them
# from; the file was a passenger.
#
# Layers are the reason this matters more than a stray file usually would. A
# secret added in one layer and deleted in a later one is still in the image, and
# an image is a thing that gets pushed, exported, cached and copied — the day
# this one goes to a registry is the day the secrets go with it, whatever the
# filesystem looks like by then.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
ops/

# Nothing below is a secret; it is weight and noise in the build context.
.git
.github
.husky
node_modules
.next
e2e
playwright-report
test-results
coverage
docs
*.log
